 The BFD 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Annelise Capossela/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Starbucks (Nasdaq: SBUX) is weighing a majority stake sale in its Japan business, which could be valued at around $3 billion, per Reuters. 

 Why it's the BFD: This highlights how the ubiquitous coffee chain seeks to eliminate distractions while trying to revitalize its core U.S. business.

 It also could spark a major bidding war in Japan, where private equity has become very active. Catch up quick: Starbucks sold a majority of its Chinese unit to Boyu Capital earlier this year at a $4 billion valuation.

 The Japan business was valued at around $1.5 billion when Starbucks bought full control from its JV partner in 2014. Starbucks stock is up 15% in 2026, but remains down nearly 15% over the past five years. The bottom line: "[CEO] Brian Niccol's efforts to lure customers back through store upgrades and heavier marketing have helped stabilize demand but have also increased costs and pressured margins. Its comparable store sales in the international segment grew 5.7% in the third quarter, with the ​company crediting Japan as a key driver." — Reuters 

 
 
