 Final Numbers 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: CoinGecko; Chart: Dan Primack/Axios The Clarity Act, the crypto industry's top policy goal, yesterday failed to advance in a key Senate procedural vote, Axios' Pete Gannon writes .

 Much of the opposition relates to the Trump family's crypto investments, and a lack of ethics rules governing them. Flashback: Coinbase chief legal officer Ryan ​VanGrack, a vet of both Citadel Securities and the Obama administration, told me in early July :

 "Clarity is on the one-yard line and I've seen nothing to suggest that the bipartisan group of senators working on it won't get it across the finish line. But to borrow a quote from Teddy Roosevelt: Nothing in the world is worth doing unless it means effort, pain, and difficulty." Look ahead: Clarity isn't dead, but it will have to wait until after the midterms — when the Senate might be even more cantankerous.

 
 
